.SH NAME
jitterentropy, jent_version \- CPU Jitter Random Number Generator
.SH LIBRARY
Jitter RNG library
.RI ( libjitterentropy ", " \-ljitterentropy ).
.BR pkg\-config (1)
knows it as
.IR jitterentropy ,
and where the library was installed by its CMake build, CMake's
.B find_package(jitterentropy)
provides the imported target
.IR jitterentropy::jitterentropy .
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <jitterentropy.h>
.sp
.BI "#define JENT_MAJVERSION x"
.sp
.BI "#define JENT_MINVERSION y"
.sp
.BI "#define JENT_PATCHLEVEL z"
.sp
.BI "#define JENT_VERSION (...)"
.sp
.BI "unsigned int jent_version(" void ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I jitterentropy
library provides a source of good entropy by collecting CPU
executing time jitter. The entropy in the CPU execution time
jitter is magnified by the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator.
The CPU Jitter Random Number Generator uses the CPU execution
timing jitter to generate a bit stream which complies with
different statistical measurements that determine the bit
stream is random.
.LP
The CPU Jitter Random Number Generator delivers entropy which
follows information theoretical requirements. Based on these
studies and the implementation, the caller can assume that
one bit of data extracted from the CPU Jitter Random Number
Generator holds one bit of entropy.
.LP
The CPU Jitter Random Number Generator provides a decentralized
source of entropy where the caller does not need to rely
on a centrally maintained source of entropy like
.IR /dev/random
or
.IR /dev/urandom .
.LP
The API is documented in these pages:
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_init (3)
the startup tests of the platform, run once per process, and the self test
of the conditioning component;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3)
allocating and freeing an entropy collector, and the flags configuring it;
.TP
.BR jent_read_entropy (3)
generating random numbers, and recovering from a health test failure;
.TP
.BR jent_status (3)
the status document, the identifier and the single values of a collector;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl (3)
the timer-less mode: its thread handler and the CPU of its counting thread;
.TP
.BR jent_set_fips_failure_callback (3)
the callback for health test failures in FIPS mode.
.SS Version
.BR JENT_MAJVERSION
indicates API / ABI incompatible changes, functional changes that require
consumer to be updated.
.LP
.BR JENT_MINVERSION
indicates API compatible, ABI may change, functional enhancements only,
consumer can be left unchanged if enhancements are not considered.
.LP
.BR JENT_PATCHLEVEL
indicates API / ABI compatible, no functional changes, no enhancements, bug
fixes only. Also, the entropy collection is not changed in any way that
would necessitate a re-assessment.
.LP
.BR JENT_VERSION
the version number of the library as an integer value that is monotonically
increasing. The version numbers are multiples of 100. For example, version
1.2.3 is converted to 1020300 -- the last two digits are reserved for
future use.
.LP
.BR jent_version ()
returns
.BR JENT_VERSION .
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_version ()
returns the
.B JENT_VERSION
of the library, which may differ from the one of the header an application
was compiled against.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
See
.B THREAD SAFETY
below for the other interfaces.
.ad l
.TS
allbox;
lbx lb
lx l.
Interface	Thread safety
T{
\%\fBjent_version\fP()
T}	MT-Safe
.TE
.ad
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The library reads no environment variables of its own. Built against
libgcrypt, it reads the one libgcrypt takes its FIPS mode from, as long as
libgcrypt is not initialized yet:
.TP
.B LIBGCRYPT_FORCE_FIPS_MODE
Set, the FIPS mode is taken as enabled, as libgcrypt will take it.
.TP
.B ALLUSERSPROFILE
On Windows, where libgcrypt 1.12 and later keep the force file below.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled
Enables the FIPS mode on Linux when it reads 1, unless the library was built
against an external crypto library, whose FIPS mode is asked instead - with
libgcrypt only once libgcrypt is initialized, until when the file is read as
libgcrypt reads it.
.TP
.I /etc/gcrypt/fips_enabled
Built against libgcrypt, and until libgcrypt is initialized: its existence
enables the FIPS mode, as it does libgcrypt's. On Windows, libgcrypt 1.12 and
later keep it as
.IR %ALLUSERSPROFILE%\eGNU\eetc\egcrypt\efips_enabled ,
which is checked as well.
.TP
.I /sys/devices/system/cpu
The data cache sizes, which the memory access noise source is sized from, and
the online CPUs on Linux.
.TP
.I /proc/cpuinfo
The core types, whose largest caches stand in on Arm systems that report
none.
.TP
.I /dev/urandom
The random identifier of an instance, on Linux where
.BR getrandom (2)
is not available and on other Unix systems without
.BR arc4random (3).
The BSDs and macOS use
.BR arc4random (3),
Windows
.BR BCryptGenRandom ().
.SH STANDARDS
The noise source and its health tests follow NIST SP 800-90B: the repetition
count test and the adaptive proportion test, extended by a repetition count
test with memory and a lag predictor test. The output is conditioned with
SHAKE-256 of FIPS 202 into an XDRBG-256. The NTG.1 mode follows the
functionality class NTG.1 of the BSI AIS 20/31 v3.0. The instance identifier
is an RFC 9562 UUID.
